---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { navListTree } from "../../demos/nav-list";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../i18n";
import { probeProp, slugId, tokens } from "../../lib/tokens";
import CopyCells from "../../components/CopyCells.astro";

const hooks = tokens
  .filter((t) => t.file === "patterns/nav-list.css")
  .map((t) => ({ ...t, prop: probeProp(t.value) }));

const import_ = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/nav-list.css";`;
const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));
---

<Base
  title="Nav list"
  description="Nav list: the destination list pattern shared by the navbar and the sidebar."
>
  <h1>Nav list</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The list of system destinations: a <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> of <code>&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a&gt;</code>, each link with an icon, a label and optional metadata at the end. Vertical in a sidebar, horizontal in a navbar.
  </p>

  <h2>Why is it a pattern and not a component</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is a single question: <em>do many components share this exact structure?</em> Here the answer is not a prediction, the navbar and the sidebar share it today, in this repo. So it sends <strong>styling hooks and</strong> structure, and <code>sk-nav-list</code> is a class that any container adds to, the same way as <code>sk-interactive</code> and <code>sk-icon</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is <strong>opt-in</strong>, like the icon and unlike the state layer: an app may not have navigation.
  </p>

  <h2>It is not a menu</h2>
  <p>
    <code>role="menu"</code> is an application menu with <code>menuitem</code> inside. This is a list of links, and the markup stays at <code>&lt;nav&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a&gt;</code>, which is what a screen reader should hear.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview
    tree={navListTree(t, { home: "/", reports: "/reports" })}
    label="Nav list"
    measure="14rem"
  />

  <h2>Vertical, more compact</h2>
  <p>
    Vertically, consecutive destinations leave no gap: each link already retains its touch area. The group label has its own top and bottom padding, to organize the sections without converting the space between items into redundant air. The horizontal variant retains its row separation.
  </p>

  <h2>The current page is not interaction</h2>
  <p>
    The hover, focus and press are from the <a href="/en/state-layer">state layer</a>, which is the only mechanism of the system, this file does not touch them. What does live here is the background of the current page, which is not interaction feedback: it persists when no one is touching it, and the state layer paints over it. And the brand is <code>aria-current="page"</code>, the one you already write; a <code>data-selected</code> of his own would be a second source for the same fact, free to contradict the first.
  </p>

  <h2>The host sends its motion</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--sk-nav-list-duration</code> and <code>--sk-nav-list-easing</code> start in the intent <em>state-change</em>, and the sidebar re-declares them with its collapse intent: so the labels fade out at the same time as the width moves. Re-declaring a pattern hook is exactly what a hook exists for, the host never touches its markup.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />

  <h2>All styling hooks</h2>
  <CopyCells />

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Hook</th>
          <th>{t("hooks.definition")} <span class="count">build</span></th>
          <th>{t("hooks.resolvedValue")} <span class="count">runtime</span></th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          hooks.map((h) => {
            const nameId = slugId("navhook-name", h.name);
            const defId = slugId("navhook-def", h.name);
            const valId = slugId("navhook-val", h.name);
            return (
              <tr>
                <td class="sk-table__cell mono sk-table__cell--copy">
                  <span id={nameId}>{h.name}</span>
                </td>
                <td class="sk-table__cell mono sk-table__cell--copy">
                  <span id={defId} data-authored>{h.value}</span>
                </td>
                <td class="sk-table__cell mono sk-table__cell--copy">
                  <span id={valId} data-token={h.name} data-prop={h.prop} data-probe="sk-nav-list">
                    …
                  </span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            );
          })
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Base>
